  "cells": [
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "source": "# The Convergence Theorems [S+N]\n\nThree theorems govern when we can swap a limit with an integral. They're the technical reason analysts moved from Riemann to Lebesgue.\n\n**Monotone convergence (MCT).** If $0 \\le f_1 \\le f_2 \\le \\cdots$ are measurable and $f_n \\uparrow f$ a.e., then\n\n$$\\int f_n \\,d\\mu \\;\\uparrow\\; \\int f\\,d\\mu.$$\n\n**Fatou's lemma.** For any sequence of non-negative measurable $f_n$,\n\n$$\\int \\liminf_n f_n\\,d\\mu \\;\\le\\; \\liminf_n \\int f_n\\,d\\mu.$$\n\n**Dominated convergence (DCT).** If $f_n \\to f$ a.e. and there's an integrable $g$ with $|f_n| \\le g$ a.e. for all $n$, then $f$ is integrable and\n\n$$\\int f_n\\,d\\mu \\;\\to\\; \\int f\\,d\\mu.$$\n\nDCT is the workhorse — most \"limits commute with integrals\" arguments in Lebesgue analysis use it.\n",
      "metadata": {}
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "source": "load(\"numerics\")$\nload(\"ax-plots\")$",
      "metadata": {},
      "execution_count": 21,
      "outputs": []
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "source": "## A Riemann counter-example that Lebesgue handles\n\nFor Riemann, swapping $\\lim$ and $\\int$ generally requires uniform convergence (Stage 1, notebook 6). Pointwise alone isn't enough. Counter-example: bump that drifts and shrinks.\n\nDefine $f_n$ on $[0, 1]$: $f_n(x) = n$ for $x \\in [0, 1/n]$, else $0$. Then:\n- $\\int_0^1 f_n\\,dx = 1$ for every $n$.\n- $f_n(x) \\to 0$ pointwise on $(0, 1]$ (and on the whole interval if we say $f(0) = 0$).\n- So $\\lim \\int f_n = 1 \\ne 0 = \\int \\lim f_n$.\n\nDCT doesn't help here because there's no integrable dominator. Fix an $x > 0$: $f_n(x) = n$ only while $n \\le 1/x$, and is $0$ after that, so $\\sup_n f_n(x) = \\lfloor 1/x \\rfloor$ — finite at every single point, but of size $\\sim 1/x$. Any dominator must beat that, and $\\int_0^1 dx/x = \\infty$. The smallest candidate is already non-integrable. Failure of DCT is *expected* — the conditions guarantee swapping; without them, anything can happen.\n",
      "metadata": {}
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "source": "/* Visualise the bump-train */\nplot_bump(n) := block([xs, ys],\n  xs : np_to_list(np_linspace(0, 1, 500)),\n  ys : map(lambda([x], if x <= 1.0/n then float(n) else 0.0), xs),\n  [xs, ys])$\n\n[xs1, ys1] : plot_bump(1)$\n[xs5, ys5] : plot_bump(5)$\n[xs20, ys20] : plot_bump(20)$\n\nax_draw2d(\n  color=\"blue\", line_width=2, name=\"n = 1\",\n  lines(xs1, ys1),\n  color=\"green\", line_width=2, name=\"n = 5\",\n  lines(xs5, ys5),\n  color=\"red\", line_width=2, name=\"n = 20\",\n  lines(xs20, ys20),\n  title=\"Bumps with mass 1 each, height → ∞, width → 0 — pointwise → 0, but ∫ = 1\",\n  xlabel=\"x\", ylabel=\"f_n(x)\",\n  grid=true, showlegend=true\n)$",
      "metadata": {},
      "execution_count": 22,
      "outputs": [
        {
